Rewrite it into canonical shape. First create-or-update the changelog singleton —
safe whether or not one already exists:

Run: `jigc doc create changelog --title Changelog --task changelog`

Then re-author every RELEASE from the foreign file, oldest to newest. For each cut
version, mint a release item (its id is minted from the version title — e.g. `1.2.0`
mints `120`). `add-item` PRINTS the new item's address; use that printed address
verbatim for every follow-up verb — never re-spell the version string:

jigc doc add-item changelog:changelog#releases --title "<version>" --task changelog
#   → prints e.g. changelog:changelog#releases/120  (call this <release-addr>)

Set the release `date` from the foreign file's HISTORICAL date — this OVERWRITES the
today-stamp `add-item` placed on create, so historical dates survive migration. Use
the date exactly as it appears in the foreign source (ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`):

jigc doc set-field <release-addr>/date --value "<historical-date>" --task changelog

If the foreign release carries a compare/diff URL, map it to the optional `link`
field (otherwise omit it — there is no other home for it and the rest of the foreign
preamble is dropped):

jigc doc set-field <release-addr>/link --value "<diff-url>" --task changelog

Under each release, add one nested change-group per category and author its notes
(one bullet per change). The category is the group's id and MUST be one of:
added / changed / deprecated / removed / fixed / security — map any foreign category
heading onto one of these (a heading outside the set is rejected at conformance).
Drive the printed address verbatim:

jigc doc add-item <release-addr>/changes --title "<category>" --task changelog
#   → prints e.g. changelog:changelog#releases/120/changes/added  (call this <group-addr>)
jigc doc set-slot <group-addr>/notes --from-file - --task changelog

For any foreign change NOT yet cut into a release (an `[Unreleased]` section), add the
change-group under the staged section instead:

jigc doc add-item changelog:changelog#unreleased-changes --title "<category>" --task changelog
#   → prints e.g. changelog:changelog#unreleased-changes/added  (call this <group-addr>)
jigc doc set-slot <group-addr>/notes --from-file - --task changelog

Validate and commit the task as one logical commit:

Run: `jigc task finalize changelog`
